> Consider
>      spiral <- data.frame(t=0:99,r=0:99)
> I would like to plot this as a four-turn spiral, that is, consider that a
> full turn of the circle is defined as t=0..25.  Put differently, use
> theta=(t %%25)*2*pi.
> I don't see how to do this straightforwardly with something like
>       ggplot(spiral) + geom_line(aes(x=t,y=r)) + coord_polar()
> because coord_polar always assumes that a full turn is equal to the range of
> x.

Neither do I !

> A workaround is to split the spiral into 'turns', like this:
>       ggplot(spiral) + geom_line(aes(x=t %%25,y=r,group=t %/% 25)) +
> coord_polar()
> but unfortunately that gives discontinuities at t %% 25 == 0.
> The simplest solution, I think, would be to have a parameter to coord_polar
> which defined the wraparound point (and also a parameter saying whether to
> connect the last point to the first).

I think the grouping approach is the right way, but coord_polar needs
to somehow now that you're working with a circular variable and the
closest path between two points can be either clockwise or
counter-clockwise.  Not sure how difficult this would be to add.

> Of course, even better would be to have ggplot itself 'understand' periodic
> data, and use more appropriate density and interpolation algorithms.

Yes, that would be great! ;)

Hadley

